\begin{align*}
\frac 2{\log_4{2000^6}} + \frac 3{\log_5{2000^6}} &= \frac 2{6\log_4{2000}} + \frac 3{6\log_5{2000}} \\
&= \frac 1{3\log_4{2000}} + \frac 1{2\log_5{2000}} \\
&= \frac{2\log_5{2000} + 3\log_4{2000}}{6\log_4{2000}\log_5{2000}}.
\end{align*}
To simplify this expression further, we can use the change of base formula for logarithms:
\begin{align*}
\frac{2\log_5{2000} + 3\log_4{2000}}{6\log_4{2000}\log_5{2000}} &= \frac{2\log{2000}/\log{5} + 3\log{2000}/\log{4}}{6\log{2000}/\log{4}\cdot \log{2000}/\log{5}} \\
&= \frac{2\log{2000}\cdot \log{4} + 3\log{2000}\cdot \log{5}}{6\log{2000}^2} \\
&= \frac{2\log{4^2} + 3\log{5}}{6\log{2000}} \\
&= \frac{2(2\log{2}) + 3\log{5}}{6\log{2000}} \\
&= \frac{4\log{2} + 3\log{5}}{6\log{2000}}.
\end{align*}
Now, we can use the fact that $\log{2000} = \log{(2^4 \cdot 5^3)} = 4\log{2} + 3\log{5}$:
\begin{align*}
\frac{4\log{2} + 3\log{5}}{6\log{2000}} &= \frac{\log{2000}}{6\log{2000}} \\
&= \boxed{\frac{1}{6}}.
\end{align*}
The answer is: \frac{1}{6}

